<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Roman Republic (1849)</h1><p class="st-meta">1849 CE – 1849 CE · capital Rome</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The republic proclaimed in Rome in 1849 and led by Mazzini, whose defence by Garibaldi against French troops became a legend of Italian unification.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX's</a> minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pellegrino-rossi">Pellegrino Rossi</a> in November 1848, the pope fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a>. An elected Constituent Assembly met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and on 9 February 1849 abolished the temporal power of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a> and proclaimed a republic.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A triumvirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-mazzini">Giuseppe Mazzini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurelio-saffi">Aurelio Saffi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-armellini">Carlo Armellini</a> governed. The republic abolished the death penalty, introduced religious freedom and drafted a democratic constitution, promulgated on 3 July 1849. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> led volunteers in defence of the city, notably on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janiculum">Janiculum</a> hill.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Louis-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sent an army under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-charles-victor-oudinot">Oudinot</a>, which besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from June and entered the city in early July 1849. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> retreated with his volunteers, and papal rule was restored under French protection.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/roman-republic-1849.jpg" alt="Roman Republic (1849)" width="320" height="184"><figcaption>Image: Ifly6 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_republic,_territory_44_BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1849 CE – 1849 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rome</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Giuseppe Mazzini, Aurelio Saffi, Carlo Armellini</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Repubblica Romana (1849)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1849&amp;focus=roman-republic-1849" aria-label="Roman Republic (1849) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4984 686 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Roman Republic (1849) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5304 942L5304 942M5304 942L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rome. <a href="/atlas?year=1849&amp;focus=roman-republic-1849">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
